MsQuic's timer wheel slots are one second wide, and an update walks its slot's sorted list.
Every connection's next timer (loss detection, pacing, ACK delay) lies within about a second, so
nearly all connections share one or two slots and each send walks thousands of entries: 25-28% of
sb-edge CPU at 20k-40k connections per shard. One-millisecond slots spread the same timers over the
wheel (which already grows to one slot per 32 connections), keeping lists short. Each slot stays
sorted and expiry scans every slot, so correctness does not depend on the slot width.
--- a/src/core/timer_wheel.c
+++ b/src/core/timer_wheel.c
@@ -63,8 +63,8 @@
 //
 // Helper to get the slot index for a given time.
 //
 #define TIME_TO_SLOT_INDEX(TimerWheel, TimeUs) \
-    ((US_TO_MS(TimeUs) / 1000) % (TimerWheel)->SlotCount)
+    (US_TO_MS(TimeUs) % (TimerWheel)->SlotCount)

 _IRQL_requires_max_(PASSIVE_LEVEL)
 QUIC_STATUS
